## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of order statistics in dynamic sets, along with practical implementation details like lazy deletion and handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts

- **Median of a dynamic set**: the middle element (or average of two middle elements) when sorted
- **Two-heap approach**: max-heap for the lower half, min-heap for the upper half
- **Lazy deletion**: defer actual removal until top operations, tracking deletions via a count map
- **Duplicate handling**: maintain frequency counts rather than storing duplicate values multiple times in the heaps

## Code Example

```javascript
class MedianFinder {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();    // max-heap for lower half
    this.high = new MinHeap();   // min-heap for upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();    // lazy deletion map
  }

  insert(num) {
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(num);
    } else {
      this.high.push(num);
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  rebalance() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  erase(num) {
    this.delayed.set(num, (this.delayed.get(num) || 0) + 1);
    if (num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.deleted++;
    } else {
      this.high.deleted++;
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  getMedian() {
    this.prune(this.low);
    this.prune(this.high);
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) {
      return this.low.peek();
    }
    return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
- Introduces a practical autocomplete scenario and a foundational DS.
- Emphasizes handling duplicates and memory management.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node pass-through counts; O(length) ops; dynamic memory.
- Duplicate handling via cnt; pruning unused nodes to bound memory.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor(){ this.next = new Map(); this.cnt = 0; }
}
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node = node.next.get(ch); node.cnt++; } }
  erase(word){ let stack=[]; let node=this.root; for(let ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return; node = node.next.get(ch); stack.push([node,ch]); } // decrement
    for(let [nd] of stack){ nd.cnt--; }
  }
  countWithPrefix(prefix){ let node=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node = node.next.get(ch); } return node.cnt; }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of dynamic order-statistics with duplicates and memory efficiency without full value-domain indexing.

## Key Concepts

- Coordinate compression
- Fenwick Tree or Segment Tree for counts
- K-th element via prefix-sum search
- Handling duplicates through counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.ft=Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  add(i,delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i & -i) this.ft[i]+=delta; }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i & -i) s+= this.ft[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){ let idx=0; let bit=1; while(bit<<1 <= this.n) bit<<=1; for(let d=bit; d; d>>=1){ let next=idx+d; if(next<=this.n && this.ft[next] < k){ idx = next; k -= this.ft[next]; } } return idx+1; }
}
```

