## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the fundamental understanding of combining data structures to achieve O(1) operations while maintaining an ordering constraint. It evaluates knowledge of hash map + linked list patterns and eviction logic commonly used in caching systems.

## Key Concepts
- HashMap for O(1) key-to-node lookups
- Doubly linked list for maintaining recency order
- Node manipulation for constant-time recency updates
- Edge case handling: missing keys, capacity limits, and duplicate operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.head = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.tail = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.head.next = this.tail;
    this.tail.prev = this.head;
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.cache.has(key)) return -1;
    const node = this.cache.get(key);
    this._moveToHead(node);
    return node.value;
  }

  put(key, value) {
    if (this.cache.has(key)) {
      const node = this.cache.get(key);
      node.value = value;
      this._moveToHead(node);
    } else {
      const newNode = { key, value, prev: null, next: null };
      this.cache.set(key, newNode);
      this._addToHead(newNode);
      if (this.cache.size > this.capacity) {
        this._removeTail();
      }
    }
  }

  _moveToHead(node) {
    this._removeNode(node);
    this._addToHead(node);
  }

  _addToHead(node) {
    node.prev = this.head;
    node.next = this.head.next;
    this.head.next.prev = node;
    this.head.next = node;
  }

  _removeNode(node) {
    node.prev.next = node.next;
    node.next.prev = node.prev;
  }

  _removeTail() {
    const node = this.tail.prev;
    this._removeNode(node);
    this.cache.delete(node.key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a dynamic, frequency-based data structure for text streams, common in search, analytics, and logs.

## Key Concepts
- Dual-map design: word→freq and freq→ordered words
- Efficient updates by relocating words between buckets
- Tie-breaking by lexicographic order
- Corner cases: zero counts, topK when fewer distinct words

## Code Example
```javascript
class WordFreqDS {
  constructor() {
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.buckets = new Map(); // freq -> Set<string>
  }
  _ensureBucket(freq) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(freq)) this.buckets.set(freq, new Set());
  }
  addWord(word) {
    const prev = this.counts.get(word) || 0;
    if (prev > 0) this.buckets.get(prev).delete(word);
    const next = prev + 1;
    this.counts.set(word, next);
    this._ensureBucket(next);
    this.buckets.get(next).add(word);
  }
  eraseWord(word) {
    const prev = this.counts.get(word);
    if (!prev) return;
    this.buckets.get(prev).delete(word);
    const next = prev - 1;
    if (next === 0) {
      this.counts.delete(word);
      return;
    }
    this.counts.set(word, next);
    this._ensureBucket(next);
    this.buckets.get(next).add(word);
  }
  getFrequency(word) {
    return this.counts.get(word) || 0;
  }
  topKWords(n) {
    const freqs = Array.from(this.buckets.keys()).sort((a,b)=>b-a);
    const res = [];
    for (const freq of freqs) {
      const words = Array.from(this.buckets.get(freq)).sort();
      for (const w of words) {
        res.push(w);
        if (res.length === n) return res;
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

